      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226336/assets/cd3af53e4d4f02c5978e8b4bc8508fb0455a0a09e7222377df68a2297bc30dae.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?",
      "worked_solution": "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=4\\cdot A=8{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$",
